.github/copilot-instructions.md
GWT to Angular 20 Migration Guidelines
Objective

Migrate the existing legacy GWT application to Angular 20.

The target application must use:

Angular 20
Standalone components
Zoneless change detection
Angular Signals for application/UI state
RxJS for asynchronous streams and HTTP operations
Strict TypeScript
Angular Router
Angular HttpClient
Automated testing
Core Migration Principle

DO NOT perform line-by-line translation from Java/GWT to TypeScript/Angular.

First understand the behavior of the existing GWT implementation.

Then implement the equivalent business behavior using appropriate Angular architecture.

The target implementation must follow Angular best practices rather than reproduce the GWT architecture.

Change Detection

The application must be zoneless.

Do not introduce:

ZoneJS
NgZone-based application logic
assumptions about automatic change detection
unnecessary ChangeDetectorRef usage

All asynchronous state that affects the UI must be represented through Angular-supported reactive mechanisms.

Signals

Use Signals extensively for:

local component state
UI state
selected items
loading state
error state
filters
pagination state
derived state

Use:

signal()
computed()
linkedSignal() where appropriate
input()
output()
model() where appropriate

Prefer computed() for derived state.

Do not use effect() merely to synchronize derived state.

Use effect() only when a genuine side effect is required.

RxJS

Use RxJS for:

HTTP operations
asynchronous streams
event streams
debouncing
cancellation
polling
complex asynchronous workflows

Do not replace every Observable with a Signal.

Use Signals for state and RxJS for asynchronous streams.

Components

Use standalone components.

Components should primarily handle:

presentation
user interaction
composition

Avoid placing large business rules directly inside components.

Services

Services should handle:

API communication
reusable business operations
external integrations
shared application functionality
State Management

Prefer the simplest state-management solution that satisfies the feature.

Use component-level Signals when state is local.

Use a feature-level store/facade when state is shared across multiple components.

Do not introduce a large global state-management framework unless there is a demonstrated requirement.

HTTP

Use Angular HttpClient.

Keep API communication outside components.

Use strongly typed request/response models.

Do not modify backend APIs unless explicitly required.

TypeScript

Use strict typing.

Avoid:

any
unnecessary type assertions
implicit types for important domain objects
duplicated interfaces
magic strings

Prefer:

interfaces
types
enums/constants where appropriate
readonly properties where appropriate
Templates

Avoid complex business logic inside templates.

Templates should remain readable.

Do not duplicate business logic between TypeScript and templates.

Error Handling

Every migrated feature must explicitly handle:

loading
success
empty state
validation errors
API errors
unexpected errors
Testing

Every migrated feature must include appropriate tests.

Tests should verify behavior rather than implementation details.

At minimum verify:

initial state
user interaction
API interaction
loading state
success state
empty state
error state
validation
navigation where applicable
Migration Process

For every GWT feature:

Analyze the existing GWT implementation.
Document the behavior.
Identify dependencies.
Identify APIs.
Identify state.
Identify events.
Create Angular migration plan.
Review the plan.
Implement the feature.
Run tests.
Run build.
Perform zoneless review.
Perform Angular architecture review.
Compare behavior with GWT.
Mark the feature as migrated.
Important Restrictions

Never blindly translate GWT classes into Angular classes.

Never assume:

GWT class = Angular component.

Instead determine the responsibility of the GWT class and map that responsibility to the appropriate Angular construct.

Examples:

GWT Widget → Angular Component

GWT Presenter → Component/Facade/Service depending on responsibility

GWT EventBus → Signals/RxJS/Component Outputs depending on usage

GWT RPC → Angular HttpClient service

GWT DTO → TypeScript interface/type

GWT Activity → Route + Component

GWT AsyncCallback → Observable/Promise

GWT History → Angular Router

Code Modification Rule

Before making significant changes:

inspect existing code
explain the intended changes
create a plan
identify impacted files

For large changes, work in small batches.

Do not modify unrelated files.

Do not refactor unrelated legacy code during feature migration.

Completion Criteria

A feature is considered migrated only when:

behavior matches the GWT implementation
Angular implementation follows the target architecture
zoneless compatibility is verified
Signals are appropriately used
RxJS is appropriately used
tests pass
build passes
lint passes
no console errors exist
code review is complete
